===Etymology===
From earlier Old English {{m|ang|wini}}, from {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*wini}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*winiz}}, whence also {{cog|odt|wini}}, {{cog|osx|wini}}, {{cog|goh|wini}}, {{cog|non|vinr}}. Ultimately from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*wenh₁-||to seek, desire, love, win}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|wine}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|m}}

# {{lb|ang|poetic}} [[friend]]

====Usage notes====
Used as a second element of many personal names. It could be appended to mythical creatures as in {{l|ang|Ælfwine||elf friend}} and {{l|ang|Entwine||giant friend}}; or animals as in {{l|ang|Lēowine||lion friend}} and {{l|ang|Wulfwine||wolf friend}}; or inanimate objects as in {{l|ang|Goldwine||gold friend}} and {{l|ang|Dūnwine||hill friend}}; or locations as in {{l|ang|Centwine||Kent friend}}; or features of nature as in {{l|ang|Sǣwine||sea friend}} and {{l|ang|Æsċwine||ash friend}}; or kinds of people as in {{l|ang|Pihtwine||Pict friend}} and {{l|ang|Bregowine||prince friend}}; or abstract concepts as in {{l|ang|Ēadwine||prosperity/happiness friend}} and {{l|ang|Bōtwine||repair/penance friend}}. It was also often used with adjectives, usually praising the owner of the name, as in {{l|ang|Beorhtwine||bright friend}} and {{l|ang|Ealdwine||old friend}}.

